<translate>
<!--T:1-->
Each file in your source directory becomes a page. {{SITENAME}} derives the page's title, and namespace, from the file's path, so the file name is how you choose them.

<!--T:2-->
== From file name to page ==

<!--T:3-->
A file ending in <code>.wikitext</code> becomes a wiki page whose title is the path with <code>.wikitext</code> removed, so <code>src/Help.wikitext</code> is the page "Help", served at <code>Help.html</code>.

<!--T:4-->
Titles are kept as written: spaces stay spaces (name the file <code>Getting Started.wikitext</code>, not with an underscore), and the first letter keeps its case. Name your entry page <code>index</code> so it builds to <code>index.html</code>, the file a static host serves at the site root.

<!--T:5-->
If a file name does not map cleanly to a title (MediaWiki would normalise it differently), the build warns, because the page's edit and history links are derived from the file name and would otherwise not resolve. Rename the file to a title that is already normalised.

<!--T:6-->
== Linking between pages ==

<!--T:7-->
Link with <code><nowiki>[[Title]]</nowiki></code>, or <code><nowiki>[[Title|label]]</nowiki></code>, the standard [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Help:Links</tvar> wikitext link]. {{SITENAME}} rewrites these to the matching <code>.html</code> paths in the static output, so internal links work with no server.

<!--T:8-->
== Subpages ==

<!--T:9-->
Put a file in a subdirectory to create a [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Help:Subpages</tvar> subpage]: <code>src/Guide/Setup.wikitext</code> becomes the page "Guide/Setup". Images in a subdirectory are imported too, but a picture is named by its file name alone -- <code>src/Guide/diagram.png</code> is <code>[[File:diagram.png]]</code>, not "Guide/diagram.png" -- so two directories cannot both hold a <code>diagram.png</code>. The build stops and names both rather than publish one page showing the other's picture. An image has to be a file rather than a link to one, for the same reason: what a link points at is not part of what you are publishing, and the build refuses it by name instead of uploading it.

<!--T:24-->
<span id="reserved-names"></span>
=== Names a subpage cannot take ===

<!--T:25-->
A subpage is a directory in the output, and some directories there are the build's own. A page that lands on one is not refused; the file is written where something else already goes, and what the reader gets is whichever finished last. Only subpages are affected, because only a subpage makes a directory: a page named <code>citizen</code> builds to <code>citizen.html</code> and sits beside everything else quite happily.

<!--T:26-->
; Any skin after the first
: Each one renders the whole site into a directory of its own, so a site building Citizen has a <code>citizen/</code> holding a second copy of every page. A page "citizen/Setup" is written into that directory, where the Citizen copy of a page "Setup" also goes. The name to avoid is the skin's own, which is lower-case and not always what you listed it by: <code>vector-2022</code>, <code>citizen</code>, <code>minerva</code>. See [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Skins</tvar>|Skins]].
; <code>assets</code>
: Everything the build generates — the stylesheets, the script bundles, and the images their CSS names — unless [[<tvar name="3">Special:MyLanguage/Configuration#WikvenAssetDirectory</tvar>|<code>WikvenAssetDirectory</code>]] names another directory, or <code>.</code> to scatter them through the output root instead.
; <code>fonts</code>
: The webfont files themselves, with <code>WikvenBundleWebfonts</code> set. They sit at the output root rather than in the asset directory, and the stylesheet that names them steps up out of it to reach them.
; <code>pagefind</code>
: Where [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Searching</tvar>|search]] writes its index, unless <code>SifterSearchOutputDir</code> puts it elsewhere.
; <code>history</code>
: Removed from the output at the end of every pass, with a line saying so. A static host has no page histories to serve, so nothing is expected to be there.

<!--T:27-->
<span id="translation-not-subpage"></span>
=== A translation is not a subpage ===

<!--T:28-->
A [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Translating</tvar>|translatable]] page -- one whose source wraps content in <code><nowiki><translate></nowiki></code> -- claims a subpage of itself named for a language <em>when that subpage carries the source's <code><nowiki><!--T:n--></nowiki></code> markers</em>. Beside a translatable <code>Guide.wikitext</code>, a marked <code>Guide/ko.wikitext</code> is the Korean translation of "Guide" and not a page "Guide/ko", and nothing warns about it, because that naming is what the feature is built on. Without the markers it is a page as written, since language codes are ordinary words too -- <code>id</code>, <code>no</code>, <code>it</code> -- and a page about identifiers should not become an Indonesian translation.

<!--T:29-->
All three have to hold at once, so any one of them is the way out: a subpage of a page carrying no <code><nowiki><translate></nowiki></code> is a subpage as written, so is any name that is not a language code, and so is any file without the markers. See [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Translating</tvar>|Translating]] for how a translation gets them.

<!--T:10-->
== Namespaces ==

<!--T:11-->
Prefix the file name with a namespace to place the page there:

<!--T:12-->
* <code>Template:Infobox.wikitext</code> is the template "Template:Infobox" (see [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Pages#templates</tvar>|Templates]]).
* <code>File:Logo.png.wikitext</code> is the description page for an uploaded <code>Logo.png</code> (see [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Images</tvar>|Images]]).
* <code>MediaWiki:Common.css</code> and <code>MediaWiki:Common.js</code> are the site-wide stylesheet and script (see [[<tvar name="3">Special:MyLanguage/JavaScript</tvar>|JavaScript]]). These keep their extension and need no <code>.wikitext</code> marker, because the name already carries the content type.

<!--T:13-->
<span id="categories"></span>
== Categories ==

<!--T:14-->
Add <code><nowiki>[[Category:Name]]</nowiki></code> to a page to [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Help:Categories</tvar> categorise] it; the categories show in the page footer, as on any wiki.

<!--T:15-->
A category's own page is not exported by default, though. {{SITENAME}} writes only ''content'' pages to the static site, and counts just the main and File: namespaces as content. (Namespaces have numeric IDs: main is <code>0</code>, File: is <code>6</code>, and Category is <code>14</code>.) So a footer category links to a <code>Category:Name.html</code> the static host does not serve, a dead link, until you export the category page. To do that, set <code>ContentNamespaces</code> in your [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Configuration#ContentNamespaces</tvar>|<code>.wikven.yaml</code> file]] and add a <code>Category:Name.wikitext</code> file to your source:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
config:
  ContentNamespaces: [0, 6, 14]
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:16-->
This key replaces the default rather than extending it, so keep <code>0</code> and <code>6</code> in the list, or the main and File: pages stop being exported.

<!--T:17-->
To keep a maintenance or tracking category out of the footer instead, add the <code><nowiki>__HIDDENCAT__</nowiki></code> [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Help:Magic_words</tvar> magic word] to its category page; a static export has no logged-in readers, so a hidden category is off the footer for everyone.

<!--T:18-->
<span id="templates"></span>
== Templates ==

<!--T:19-->
Put a <code>Template:Name.wikitext</code> file in your source and use it on any page as <code><nowiki>{{Name}}</nowiki></code>. For example, this file:
</translate>

<translate nowrap>
<!--T:22-->
<syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" copy>
'''Note:''' <tvar name="1">{{{1}}}</tvar>
</syntaxhighlight>
</translate>

<translate>
<!--T:20-->
saved as <code>Template:Note.wikitext</code> and used on a page as:
</translate>

<translate nowrap>
<!--T:23-->
<syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" copy>
<tvar name="1">{{Note|</tvar>Rebuild the site after every edit.<tvar name="2">}}</tvar>
</syntaxhighlight>
</translate>

<translate>
<!--T:21-->
renders as "'''Note:''' Rebuild the site after every edit." Templates take positional (<code><nowiki>{{{1}}}</nowiki></code>) and named parameters and run [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Help:Parser_functions</tvar> parser functions] exactly as in MediaWiki. The syntax itself is standard MediaWiki; see [<tvar name="2">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Help:Templates</tvar> Help:Templates].
</translate>

